1 INTRODUCTION
This project cover research and exploration of interaction design with an Augmented Reality Head-Up Display (AR-HUD), developing navigation features. Cars are the leading mean of transportation in personal mobility, and the number of cars in Europe reached 242,7 million in 2019 (Acea, 2021). The automotive industry is evolving towards a future of electrification, autonomous vehicles, and connectivity, creating a need to explore user experience and technology innovation (Hermann & Singh, 2020). The evolution of automotive technology has made the development of Human-Machine Interfaces (HMI) have user-centered approaches as the standard considering user perspectives in achieving usable systems (François, Osiurak, Fort, Crave, & Navarro, 2016).
The project is carried out in Gothenburg at Volvo Cars, and Volvo Cars have a vision of zero people getting injured or killed in a new Volvo car (Volvo Cars, n.d.). The project aims to explore the user experience and interaction with the navigation feature in AR-HUD, including investigating if better perceptual and safety aspects can be achieved with the AR-HUD technology. When exploring the user interaction, graphics were designed, implemented in a concept car, and evaluated with users. The work was evaluating whether navigation in ARHUD can be used safely and correctly and if it could contribute to a safer driving environment and less cognitive workload for drivers.
This project is a master’s thesis in Industrial design engineering at Lulea University of Technology in the spring of 2022. The project is a part of the SCREENS1 research project carried out by Volvo Cars and the Research Institute of Sweden (RISE). The goal is to contribute with industrial design engineering perspectives to the SCREENS projects’ future research about implementation of AR-HUDs in cars which include the user experiences for HMI-design.
1.1 BACKGROUND
Drivers’ information is historically positioned on the dashboard in cars forcing drivers to direct attention away from the road, which could ultimately cause accidents (Lee, Koo, Park, & Lee, 2021). To provide the necessary information without distracting the drivers’ attention, manufacturers started implementing Head-Up Displays (HUDs) projecting 2-dimensional (2D) visual information. This information could be recognized quickly and reduce distractions from the road. Efforts to create graphics of the surrounding road environment were found hard using 2D graphics. Lee et al. (2021) argues that 3-dimensional (3D) graphics projecting virtual images of the real environment became possible when Augmented Reality (AR) started to be explored in HUDs. AR, a relatively new technology (Boboc, Gîrbacia, & Butilă, 2020), created possibilities to integrate virtual information and objects into real environments (Himperich, 2007).
The first AR applications were video-based screens that presented a video with AR overlays, and later technological innovation progressed into see-through windshield applications (Gabbard, Fitch, & Kim, 2014). With technological advancements, the most successful ARHUD solutions are believed to be developed by those who consider perceptual and attentional aspects in their development (Gabbard et al., 2014). Where to present information, what information to include, how users interact with the system, how to measure usability, and
