1.2 STAKEHOLDERS
The project’s primary internal stakeholders are Volvo Cars and RISE. They are the primary beneficiaries of conclusions contributing to the SCREENS projects parallel research.
External stakeholders are people in a traffic environment, both the driver operating vehicles and pedestrians. This project has a user-centered approach, which implies that external stakeholders are included in the process e.g., evaluating design concepts.
1.3 OBJECTIVES AND AIMS
In an innovative environment like the automotive industry, exploring new technological implementations is crucial if it can benefit the user and gain competitiveness.
The project objective is to research, ideate, implement, and evaluate a user interface design for a navigation feature in an AR-HUD. Existing technological limitations, human perception, and cognitive psychology are considered throughout the development process to develop a feasible concept to implement and evaluate.
The project aims to design and evaluate the user interaction in an AR-HUD to achieve good user experiences and evaluate whether AR-HUD contributes to safer driving. The aim can be divided into two parts: part one aim to evaluate visual information designs. The second part evaluates user experience with drivers operating an AR-HUD car regarding perception, attention, and safety. The results of the project aim to contribute with valuable insights for future research within the subject of AR-HUDs from an industrial design engineering perspective.
Three research questions to be answered were formulated for this project:
1) How can information be designed and implemented to be perceived correctly and safely in an AR-HUD?
2) What information is suitable for drivers regarding a navigation feature in an AR-HUD?
3) How does AR-HUD affect driver attention and distraction?
1.4 PROJECT SCOPE
The project was performed with a human-centered design approach to evaluate the user experience for interactions with a car equipped with AR-HUD. The concept car used in this project were a Volvo XC60 equipped with an AR-HUD system from the company Envisics. Therefore, the project process could involve users at Volvo Cars to evaluate user experience and interaction with the system in a realistic environment. The focus was the interaction between driver and car, and the primary user involvement occurred in testing, but to some extent in the development of graphics.
