A definition, without limitations of technology, of Augmented Reality has the following characteristics (Azuma, 1997; Rebbani, Azougagh, Bahatti, & Bouattane, 2021):
1) Combination of virtual and real objects in a real-world environment. 2) Interactions in real time. 3) Both virtual and real objects align and are registered in three dimensions.
AR is a variation of Virtual Reality (VR) (Azuma, 1997). In VR, users are completely immersed in a virtual environment and cannot experience their surroundings. In AR, the user can experience the real world with virtual graphics superimposing the real world (Azuma, 1997; Rebbani et al., 2021). AR supplements reality with virtual graphics without replacing it, enabling the real world and digital objects to be perceived as co-existing (Rebbani et al., 2021). AR helps users notice information from their surroundings with digital information enriching objects that users cannot notice directly and help perform tasks in the real world (Azuma, 1997).
2.5 HEAD-UP DISPLAYS
A Head-Up Display (HUD) is a display system superimposing virtual images upon a seethrough display in the users’ field of view, either the windshield or a separate combiner (Stevens, Quimby, Board, Kersloot, & Burns, 2002). Historically, first being implemented in fighter jets for pilots to have information presented in their field of view (Rizov, Kjosevski, & Tashevski, 2017). The automotive industry first saw a HUD implemented into a car in 1988 when an Oldsmobile Cutlass Supreme was equipped with a HUD (Pauzie, 2015; Rizov et al., 2017). Interest from manufacturers and users was back then low due to issues with the technology. It has taken until recent years for the maturity and advancements of HUD technology to be sufficient for the vehicle industry to gain interest (Pauzie, 2015).
Advantages with HUD technology are reducing the need to adjust views between the road and displays on the dashboard and other parts of the vehicle interior. This reduces the need for drivers to adjust eye focus between information displays and the road and increases time keeping their eyes on the road (Rizov et al., 2017).
2.5.1 ENVISICS AR-HUD TECHNOLOGY Technology from Envisics installed in the concept car has their Dynamic Holographic platform, which can simultaneously project graphics displayed at multiple distances, enabling AR graphics to be projected at a further virtual image distance than conventional HUD technology (Envisics, 2022). The virtual image distance of Envisics technology is 8 to 10 meters (Envisics, 2022), whereas conventional HUDs have an image depth of 2 to 3 meters (Lee, et al., 2020), see Figure 1. A further virtual image distance help reducing the eyes frequently refocusing (Deng, Ye, Chen, & Yang, 2021).
Other HUD systems have a loss of more than 90% of illumination during projection. This wastes energy and creates heat, whereas with their technology that harnesses and controls lasers, it utilizes these wastes, and losses are decreased while generating high levels of clarity, depth, and saturation of the imagery (Envisics, 2022).
