2.6 AUTOMOTIVE NAVIGATION SYSTEMS
The main objective of automotive navigation systems is to guide drivers from location A to destination B (Schaub, Hipp, Kargl, & Weber, 2013). It utilizes Global Positioning Systems (GPS) that collect geographical coordinates of objects on the earth’s surface and present an exact location and velocity in real time (Hofmann-Wellenhof, Lichtenegger, & Collins, 2012).
Different features are integrated into HMI systems to communicate the route during guidance (Schaub, et al., 2013). Most systems have three primary display sources: an electronic map, turn-by-turn directions, and a list of maneuvers (Lavie, Oron-Gilad, & Meyer, 2011). Electronic maps are used to visually represent areas around the vehicle where the route is indicated with color, and some cursor indicates the vehicle. When driving on the given route, the display scrolls down, showing future navigation on the map. Users should only need to glance at electronic maps and other navigation displays when searching for guidance, which gives importance to systems designed to give information clearly and easily perceived (Lavie, et al., 2011).
2.7 COMPETITOR ANALYSIS
Today there are few existing solutions for an AR-HUD in cars implemented on the market. Two manufacturers have launched car models with AR-HUD as an option: Mercedes Benz (Mercedes Benz, n.d.) and Volkswagen (Volkswagen, 2021). In addition to these manufacturers, other car manufacturers and technological companies also develop conceptual solutions of AR-HUD systems. Skoda and Peugeot (PSA), and the technological developers AGC Glass and Envisics has created concepts for AR-HUD.
