3 THEORETICAL FRAMEWORK
This section of the project presents the theoretical framework of the field of industrial design engineering, user experience design, human visual senses, and visual perception. This section aims to give insight and knowledge of challenges and possibilities for an AR-HUD and underlying factors that affect interaction with the technology.
3.1 INDUSTRIAL DESIGN ENGINEERING
This master’s thesis project is within the field of industrial design engineering and this section presents a description of industrial design engineering.
Product development is an interdisciplinary process having contributors from different disciplines and knowledge (Kim & Lee, 2010). Product development has needed design engineers to create functional solutions for the product, while industrial designers were responsible for the outside appearance (Kim & Lee, 2010). When product development started focusing more on human and market needs, the user experience became a critical factor in product development (Persson & Warell, 2003). Industrial design engineers have interdisciplinary knowledge in both design engineering and industrial design, being familiar with a complete product development process (De Vere, Melles, & Kapoor, 2010). They know engineering science, technology, and mechanics combined with psychology, arts, and craft (Schaefer, Eckert, & Coates, 2019). Design is described as making sense of things (Krippendorff, 1989), and balancing theory and practice (Buchanan, 2001). Buchanan (2001) describe design as:
“Design is the human power of conceiving, planning, and making products that serve human beings in the accomplishment of their individual and collective purposes.” (p.9)
Design, when described as making sense (of things), create a paradox between creating the product and making sense of it to be understandable and recognizable (Krippendorff, 1989). A circular cognitive process aiming to make sense of things includes shifting from an incomprehensible perception of information into trying to understand a hypothetical context until it converges into a reasonable understanding of the information perceived (Krippendorff, 1989).
3.2 USER EXPERIENCE DESIGN
This section describes the field of User Experience (UX) design as this project has a UX design approach, involving users to get feedback on the interaction with the product. UX design has become a fundamental approach in the product development of interactive products over the last decade (Hassenzahl & Tractinsky, 2006).
According to Norman (2013), one of the most remembered aspects of using products are the interactions, making it essential when designing for human use. He proceeds that interaction errors should never be blamed on the user but instead on designers not considering human physical-, psychological-, and emotional needs in product development. Hassenzahl & Tractinsky (2006) imply that today interactive products have matured from useful and usable into fashionable and desired by users. They mean that UX has had the purpose of preventing usability problems but is shifting towards bringing quality to life and has, therefore, shifted beyond interaction with products. They define modern UX design from the three perspectives in Figure 3.
9

Figure 3: Modern UX-design perspectives. Inspired by Hassenzahl & Tractinsky (2006) pp.95. Illustrated by Johan Hansols.
The first perspective is designing beyond the instrumental, which suggests looking beyond usability testing and measuring the task-based performance of products (Hassenzahl & Tractinsky, 2006). This perspective considers aesthetics and hedonic (pleasurable) aspects an important part of the user experience. It fulfills underlying human needs of stimulation, personal growth, and perfection of skills and knowledge to gain non-instrumental aspects that create more holistic design experiences (Hassenzahl & Tractinsky, 2006).
Emotional and affectional aspects of products are the second aspect Hassenzahl & Tractinsky (2006) say is influential on today's UX design. Some product interactions come automatically for one user while bringing positive emotions to others. Hassezahl & Tractinsky (2006) bring up the example of a key bowl inside the entrance door of a home. The father might put the keys in without much thought, while the children might get positive emotions of being home when seeing their father put them there. This example displays the effects of products linked to emotional and affectional rituals. They mean that by including human affection and emotions as a consequence and mediator of interaction with a product, designers can create interactive products by giving great experiences. Designing for positive emotions and affection might be hard to control, but considering user emotions and affection can set the context for positive emotion and affect (Hassenzahl & Tractinsky, 2006).
The third aspect of UX design is experiential. It is a temporary and situated experience based on the product and users' internal states, e.g., the users' expectations, mood, and goal (Hassenzahl & Tractinsky, 2006). Combinations of these elements modify each other and create a process with a start and end, creating the experience. This means that products influence users differently depending on situation, e.g., looking at their nice car in the garage versus what they feel driving the car on a beautiful road in beautiful weather. The experiential aspect thus implies the situated and temporary effect of users’ experiences. Considering this in product development, rather than viewing interactive products as transparent, blending in and hardly recognized can elevate user interactions. Judgment of experiences and the actual experiences are seen as related but not the same (Hassenzahl & Tractinsky, 2006).
10

3.3 HUMAN-MACHINE INTERFACES
Theory of Human-machine interface (HMI) design and interactions with HMI´s are highly relevant for this project since the interaction between driver and the car (AR-HUD) is what this thesis aims to explore.
People and machines are very different, where people are creative, versatile, and flexible (Norman, 2013). On the other hand, machines are precise, inflexible, and static in operations, which creates a mismatch that can be beneficial if implemented correctly (Norman, 2013). Good HMI´s are when humans and machines work as a collaborative system in symbiosis with each other (Osvalder & Ulfvengren, 2015; Norman, 2013). HMI systems can be built up as a cycle, where humans and machines interact to solve tasks in different contexts, see Figure 4 (Osvalder & Ulfvengren, 2015).
Figure 4: Cycle of an HMI process. Illustrating interaction and bridges between human and machine. Inspired by Osvalder & Ulfvengren (2015) pp.401. Illustrated by Johan Hansols.
Norman (2013) means there are voids that system design must create bridges between humans and machines in this cycle. He calls these the gulf of execution and the gulf of evaluation that connects humans and machines in the cycle. For HMI´s to create purpose for users, the system must have good usability, meaning it has relevant and working functions and is easy and safe to operate and understand for users (Osvalder & Ulfvengren, 2015). Creating the bridge of execution, the user interface design must inform the user of the actions available in an understanding way (Norman, 2013). The bridge of evaluation needs to display feedback on the results of the actions, giving users easily perceivable and accurate representations of the system's status. The user can then act according to their goals (Norman, 2013). Problems occurring at the gulf of execution, Osvalder & Ulfvengren (2015) say, are when the operators' mental goals do not translate into action and when the operators' goals are clear. However, they do not understand how to execute them. When these problems happen, the operator must think of what he or she can do and what it leads to, what possible actions there are, and if the action gets them closer to their goal. At the gulf of evaluation, Osvalder & Ulfvengren (2015) say that problems are difficult to evaluate if the machine's information output corresponds to a mental goal and the operator sees the information but does not know what it is. For these issues, they mean it is the designers' responsibility to question whether the information is understandable, if it gives a good representation of the machine's status, and if the information aligns with reality.
11

3.4 HUMAN INFORMATION PROCESSING
Standard theories about information processing suggest it is a one-directional process, whereas the modern model suggests that components constantly interact with each other (Ashcraft & Radvansky, 2009). This section presents a general model of modern information processing and its components, see Figure 5. The components covered are the perceptual encoding and central processing. These components include theory about how visual information is acquired, the process of encoding the information, human attentional resources, and how the information is stored in memory stores.
Figure 5: A model for human information processing. Inspired by Wickens et al., (2013) pp.102. Illustrated by Johan Hansols.
3.4.1 ACQUIRING VISUAL INFORMATION Human vision is the most dominating sense for perceived input from our surroundings (Wade & Swanston, 2013). Human information processing starts with the eyes processing enormous amount of visual information and compressing bits of information from 120 million to about 1 million before reaching the cognitive system (Ashcraft & Radvansky, 2009). The eye receives light waves focused and inverted by the lens and then projected onto the Retina, see Figure 6. Most accurate and precise vision is achieved on the Fovea, and light hitting the Retina twenty degrees from the Fovea results in the peripheral vision. From the Retina, signals are transported toward further cognitive processing via optical (Ashcraft & Radvansky, 2009).
Figure 6: Anatomy of the eye. Inspired by Ashcraft & Radvansky (2009) pp.67. Illustrated by Johan Hansols.
12

3.4.2 DECODING INFORMATION After the amount of information has been processed through the eyes, the cognitive systems start encoding input into a usable mental form. The human mental processes have parallel processing of different stimuli simultaneously called parallel processing (Ashcraft & Radvansky, 2009). This is a rapid process where features, orientations, colors, textures, and movement patterns are extracted in a bottom-up data-driven fashion from the environment (Ware, 2019).
Following this rapid first stage of encoding visual information is the pattern processing, where a slower process of dividing the information based on simple patterns, similar colored regions, continuous contours, or similar textured regions. Creating small groups of similar entities of information then either goes on to parts of the action system or the object recognition. The last stage is visual cognition, where construction of the information and patterns perceived is formed from visual queries to be answered from memory stores (Ware, 2019).
3.4.3 MEMORY In human information processing, two memory stores are involved: Short-term memory (STM) and Long-term memory (LTM) (Ashcraft & Radvansky, 2009). STM is a temporary and limited memory store only holding information active in the cognitive process for short periods (Wickens, Lee, Liu, & Gordon-Becker, 2013). It is the memory containing either perceived information or retrieved information from LTM, waiting to be further mentally processed. The time that information remains in STM decay over time but can be reactivated when rehearsed. An optimistic definition made by researchers is that capacity in STM is no more than 7 ±2 chunks of information at once. Chunks are created when associating different information with each other, and these associations can be based on familiarity and past knowledge. Chunks help the capacity of STM to increase and make the information more likely to be transferred to the LTM. In LTM, information is no longer active where it can be stored for a longer time and retrieved later (Wickens et al., 2013). LTM stores inactive information learned from experiences and stores it in categories (Osvalder & Ulfvengren, 2015). Information in LTM is essential in perceiving information because of abilities to reference perceived stimuli to stored information in LTM. It helps when decoding perceived information and is particularly important when inadequate stimuli are perceived (Osvalder & Ulfvengren, 2015).
3.4.4 ATTENTION In traffic environments, some events cause minimal distraction to the driver’s attention, while others distract attention making their ability to drive the car suffer (Ojsteršek & Topolšek, 2019). Driver’s attention is influenced by mind wandering, daydreaming, decision making, and usage of vehicle systems, e.g., radio or navigation, all connected to human cognitive processes. These influences can reduce driver performance and is one cause of accidents in traffic (Ojsteršek & Topolšek, 2019).
The human resources for attention are limited, and the more scattered the attention is, the quality of how the surrounding is understood gets worse (Osvalder & Ulfvengren, 2015). Depending on the stimuli and the driver’s interests, motives, and experience, the driver can direct attention toward what is important. Two essential types of attention are, selectiveand shared attention.
13

Osvalder & Ulfvengren (2015) mean that Selective attention is the ability to distribute the cognitive capacity by focusing on particular information for brief moments. Further suggesting that there are four factors to what information is attended to:
- How prominent and clear is the information: Flashing information, and sudden appearances can be hard to overlook. - What the individual expects to appear: expectations originate from past experiences, and individuals search for information where it is expected to be found. - The information’s value to the individual: the information needs to be relevant, useful, and important for the individual. - Effort needed to gather the information: if the information is demanding to gather, the individual might overlook it. It is dependent on the size of the selection when selecting one out of three is done effortlessly, while one out fifty is far more demanding.
Shared attention Osvalder & Ulfvengren (2015) suggests is the human ability to attend to several sources of information simultaneously without loss of information. They say that the ability to attend to a more significant number of information sources is easier with more experience when, e.g., driving a car. An experienced driver can perform secondary tasks, even if driving in poor weather and rush hour traffic. In contrast, a novice driver solely needs to focus on the surroundings and drive safely. Osvalder & Ulfvengren (2015) signifies three factors for shared attention:
- How much effort does the primary task, e.g., driving a vehicle, demand from the induvial. Driving a vehicle demand less attention from experienced drivers. - Similarities of attention needed. If information demands activation of the same senses in response to stimuli, e.g., two stimuli needing visual attention are difficult to attend to at the same time. - How much switching between information sources is needed.
These types of attention summarize human attention as selective attention that help individual attend to certain stimuli and shared attention that attends to the number of stimuli that can be processed (Osvalder & Ulfvengren, 2015).
3.5 SITUATION AWARENESS
Situation awareness (SA) is vital for drivers to perceive the environment around them and understand the situation, both in the moment and in the near future, to make appropriate actions leading to their goal (Endsley, Bolté, & Jones, 2003). Automotive information system design should support SA in communicating environmental information to drivers in an easily perceivable way. Traffic environments carry complex and dynamic situations making decisions in traffic dependent on drivers' awareness of what the situation around them looks like. In these situations, SA is a driver for decision making and performance. Endsley et al. (2003) say that SA is a constantly changing image of the environmental state. If systems are designed with a high level of SA, the users can more easily achieve their objectives with the system. They say that SA is a goal-oriented process meaning that objects in the environment are based on the goal or job set out to achieve. Therefore, designing systems with high SA must have a goal-directed methodology to know what data users must be aware of. SA directly supports the cognitive process of the users with it being a key aspect of decision making, and often interaction errors occur because users misunderstand the situation of use (Endsley et
14

al., 2003). In the cognitive process, distributed cognition is a microanalysis of situations when performing tasks (Preece, et al., 1994). In this process of microanalysis, SA is of importance when human senses pick up glances and audible cues from the states of the situation.
Endsley et al. (2003) mean that fundamental for designing systems with high SA is keeping users in control. Users having control over the system and simultaneously at high levels of SA will result in usable and user-centered designs.
3.6 VISUAL PERCEPTION
The visual system can perceive objects in bright light and the darkest of night and rapidly moving or disappearing objects. There are different theories of how humans perceive things, including constructive- and ecological perception. The two theories disagree on the process of perceiving things (Preece, et al., 1994).
3.6.1 CONSTRUCTIVE PERCEPTION THEORY In constructive perception theory, Gregory (2015) says that information is influenced by representations and memories constructing mental models of perceived information from retinal images. This conception implies that humans create hypotheses based on memories and representation when perceiving visual information (Gregory, 2015). The constructive approach suggests that when perceiving a text, humans can perceive the text “THE CAT” differently, see Figure 7. Even though the middle letter in the two words is the same, it is perceived as an H and A because of the context it is perceived in (Preece, et al., 1994).
Figure 7: Illustration of how the same letter H is perceived as an H in "THE" and an A in "CAT" because of the context we perceive and read it in. Inspired by Preece et al. (1994) pp. 78. Illustrated by Johan Hansols.
3.6.2 ECOLOGICAL PERCEPTION THEORY The ecological theory for visual perception is the idea of perception being a straightforward process rather than being a mental process in the brain (Gibson, 1979). The theory suggests that humans actively explore environments and understand how to interact with them from objects, events, or systems (Gibson, 1979). Knowing if objects of the environment are possible to interact with, easily perceived affordance becomes a link between perception and action, while poor affordance leads to failed interactions (Gaver, 1991). Therefore, affordance is central in the ecological approach to visual perception because it suggests that when perceiving the world, humans are actively looking for ways to interact with objects (Gaver, 1991). An everyday example of the ecological approach and perceived affordance is door handles. How people perceive them and whether to push or pull, see Figure 8. The vertical handle affords pulling, while the horizontal affords pushing (Gaver, 1991; Norman, 2013).
15

Figure 8: Affordance of different door handles suggesting different actions. Inspired by Gaver (1991) pp. 80. Illustrated by Johan Hansols.
3.6.3 GESTALT GROUPING PRINCIPLES Utilizing gestalt grouping principles when designing visual information for HMI´s can be a good way of designing comprehensible interfaces.
The most basic principle is the figure-ground principle. It implies that when perceiving an image, part of the image is considered in the foreground (the object) and segregated from parts which the object is set upon (the background) (Ashcraft & Radvansky, 2009). Gestalt psychologists researched gestalt laws of the perceptual organization during the middle of the 20th century (Preece, et al., 1994). The psychologist of the time believed that the interpretation of objects and scenes could be understood from pattern- and organizational laws during perception. These laws were a consequence of the constructive theory of perception. The perceiver can interpret information based on prior knowledge from specific patterns or organizations of shapes from visual stimuli. These principles were defined as proximity, similarity, closure, good continuation, and symmetry, see Figure 9. The laws suggests that symmetry group elements close together, and similarity group similar elements (Lidwell, Holden, & Butler, 2010). Closure is perceived as elements in a set are together in the context. Good continuity are elements placed in formation of a curve and are perceived as grouped. Symmetrical elements are perceived as a unity when mirrored (Lidwell, Holden, & Butler, 2010).
Implementing the principles, patterns and organizations can be perceived as a whole and give meaning and understanding (Preece, et al., 1994). Another principle is Common fate which means that elements that move in the same direction is perceptually grouped (Lidwell, Holden, & Butler, 2010; Ashcraft & Radvansky, 2009). For example, if objects are move in an environment with other static objects, it is easier to perceive as a grouped object than if it remains motionless (Ashcraft & Radvansky, 2009). Common fate can be applied for grouping moving- and flickering objects if the elements' velocity and frequency are consistent (Lidwell, Holden, & Butler, 2010).
Figure 9: Visualization of gestalt laws for an organization. Inspired by Preece et.al. (1994). Illustrated by Johan Hansols.
16

Easterby (1970) suggests that pattern graphics are perceived differently if composed of outlines or solid colors when creating guiding graphics. He means that by creating “figural goodness” when designing graphics, they are easier to perceive. When having solid graphics they are perceived as contrasting to the background resulting in an easy perceivable element compared to outlines. Easterby (1970) also say that solid elements are perceived in the foreground of less important outlined graphics when designing functional graphics.
