4 METHOD AND IMPLEMENTATION
In this section, an explanation of the development process and the methodology used is presented. The section presents the methods used, and how they were implemented in this project.
4.1 PROCESS
The process of this project follows a slightly modified version of the iterative cycle of humancentered design. Typically having stages of observation, ideation, prototyping, and testing that conclude one iteration cycle. These stages are then repeated until the results are satisfactory (Norman, 2013). There are no templates for specific problems to solve, and all design strategies need to consider the nature of the problem (Wikberg Nilsson, Ericson, & Törlind, 2015). For this project, the stages were modified into ideate, implement, test, and analyze because of the unfamiliar technology making user observation difficult, see Figure 10. The process included users during evaluations to get feedback of the concepts created before going into next iteration. The iterative process was chosen because the right solutions are often not found directly (Preece, Rogers, & Sharp, 2015). The first iteration of the project focused on understanding the context of the technology, ideating, and evaluating different designs of navigation information graphics. Following the first iteration, the focus was directed to refinement of graphics and evaluating perceptual, safety, and attentional aspects with users. During these two iteration cycles, testing concepts with users focused on evaluating different aspects of the interaction with the system. The first iteration evaluated the designs of different graphics regarding the interpretation of actions to make, visibility toward the environment, and what graphics are desirable in the AR-HUD. This evaluation aimed to give insights of how to design visual information before moving on to the next iteration that focused on evaluating the user experience when using the system in a realistic environment.
Figure 10: Iterative cycle of human-centered design (modified). Inspired by Norman (2013) pp. 222. Illustrated by Johan Hansols.
