4.3.2 TRY IT YOURSELF The technique Try it yourself let designers and researchers pose as the user and explore firsthand what needs there might be for the product area (Think Design, n.d.). Researchers can put themselves in users’ shoes and explore the context and experience the same actions or things as users do, gaining empathy for users, defining problem areas, and understanding user behavior and needs without having to make assumptions (Think Design, n.d.).
4.4 LITERATURE REVIEW
Milton & Rodgers (2013) say that writing comprehensive literature reviews can be summarized into five important steps. Beginning a review, the first step is defining questions or problem areas to research. Second step is data collection which begins when the problem statement and field of research is defined. Where to collect data from and how to collect it is critical to cover as much of the topic as possible. The third step is evaluating the data and sources, gaining an understanding of the data, and defining the sources credibility and authenticity. After collecting and evaluating data, the fourth step is analyzing and interpreting the data to understand the most important information about the source’s topics. Lastly, a literature review needs a structured presentation of the data gathered. It gives a rich overview of the subject and displays structure when presenting the issues, objectives, and different opinions on the researched subject (Milton & Rodgers, 2013).
Performing the literature review in this project, research was done thoroughly within several different subject fields. Research fields and search words were industrial design engineering, user experience design, human-machine interfaces, head-up displays, augmented reality, augmented reality head-up display, human-computer interface, visual perception, and situation awareness. Sources to collect data for reviewing the topics were scientific articles, journals, books, and conference proceedings collected from the university database, Google scholar, and theoretical literature. The evaluation of sources was done through looking at the number of citations the sources had and evaluating where the publication was made. Sources relating to the automotive industry were collected to the best extent. Other industries were also relevant to research to get different perspectives on the same topic.
4.5 FIRST ITERATION - IDEATE
This iteration emphasizes exploring ideas on how visual information could be designed and evaluated with users. The project's first iteration aims to explore different graphics designs to guide drivers on their route to their destination.
4.5.1 SKETCHING While exploring the context and working on this project, pen and paper have always been beside the keyboard for the opportunity to sketch ideas that pop up quickly. It enabled remembering ideas for later.
Some say a “conversation” between mind and sketch is created from knowledge and interpreted from the resulting representation, creating new knowledge (Buxton, 2010). Others argue it is a way of self-reporting thoughts or restructuring ideas (Verstijnen, van Leeuwen, Goldsmith, Hamel, & Hennessey, 1998). Pen and pencil sketches are intuitive, rapid, and often vague and unspecified, with the possibility of creatively reconstructing new representations of perceived sketches (Verstijnen et al., 1998).
20

Sketching was used as a self-reporting and visualization tool to quickly get thoughts and ideas down and documented. When exploring the context of the project, many ideas of came to mind when exploring different topics. Later, it was easy to look at those ideas, re-iterate, or digitalize them by quickly sketching them on paper.
4.5.2 IDEA GENERATING WORKSHOPS Ideation workshops with people from different backgrounds were held during the ideation process, which is a great way to generate ideas and further explore the problem area.
The primary purpose of having a workshop is to invite people outside the design team to explore a specific problem area or subject and get an outside view of the problem (Wikberg Nilsson, Ericson, & Törlind, 2015). Planning should start by setting the agenda, rules to follow, roles, and an expected time needed (Methodkit, n.d.). What problem settings, conditions that apply, and possible outcomes should be considered to understand resources needed, people to invite, and how to design the contents (Wikberg Nilsson et al., 2015). An ice breaker exercise is an excellent start to the workshop to achieve a creative mindset and comfortable setting. Then exploring the present gives an understanding of the problem area. When starting to ideate possible solutions, a “how might we...?” and “what if…” mindset helps with creativity. Before ending the workshop, a summary of what has been achieved should be presented (Wikberg Nilsson, et al., 2015).
The exercise Flip book aims to acquaint everyone with each other and warm up before creative sessions (Lindqvist, 2020). The exercise aims to produce an introductory flipbook introducing everyone to each other using post-its. Nine questions are to be answered either by a sketch or text. Questions easy to sketch are described with text and vice versa. Thirty seconds per question make participants think and act quickly. Either let everyone present their flip book or let someone else do it (Lindqvist, 2020).
Creating ideas was the main objective of the workshop sessions, and two variations of brainstorming were done to achieve it. Brainstorming means using the brain to storm creativity to solve a problem, and it is one of the most well-known and used methods for creating ideas (Wilson, 2013; Osborn, 1953). It was invented by Osborn back in the 1940´s and 1950`s, but the main principles of the method are still used today (Wilson, 2013). Judgment is ruled out during the session, wild ideas are encouraged, quantity of ideas is wanted, and combinations or improvements of ideas is sought (Osborn, 1953; Wikberg Nilsson, et al., 2015). The facilitator should start by defining the theme, stating the rules, and explaining what is being ideated (Wikberg Nilsson, et al., 2015). Ideas are sketched or written on post-its or paper, and sessions are better kept short because when ideas run dry, the session ends or transitions to the next step.
The workshop was held on three separate days in this project, with four people the first day and two people on the last two occasions. Participants had hardware, software, and design backgrounds. The purpose was to explore graphics design ideas beyond the traditional arrows often used in navigation. Starting by introducing the focus area of local guidance, guiding drivers to position their car correctly, but trying not to influence participants with too much information about existing solutions. Before starting the workshop, the agenda was introduced, see Appendix A.
21

Nine questions were planned for a fun and creative introductory exercise when starting with the Flipbook ice breaker. Following the questions, each participant presented the flip book of the person to the left to end the warm-up exercise. The ideation was divided into parts of reversed brainstorming and traditional brainstorming. The reversed brainstorm aimed to think outside the box, and ideas of how not to design guiding graphics were sketched and presented, opening for discussion. Attempting to solve the problem was initially sketching and writing ideas on post-its. Templates of a driver's view of the road were created to ease the sketching of ideas after the first session, see Figure 12. Scenarios of turning, changing lanes, holding the lane, and going into a roundabout served as the base for generating ideas. Results were presented and discussed before ending the workshop.
