4.7 FIRST ITERATION - IMPLEMENT
Implementation of graphics in this iteration used a trial-and-error approach. Sketched ideas were digitalized and tried both in a simulation website and in the concept car. This way, the graphics could be viewed in the real environment of the implementation. 4.7.1 DIGITALIZING GRAPHICS Digitalizing the three concept ideas from ideation were done with Adobe Illustrator and Photoshop. Illustrator can enhance drawings with 3D effects (Adobe, 2022). Since AR graphics are supposed to enhance a 3D environment, it was a great tool. Creating animated graphics, frames were created with Illustrator and exported to Photoshop as an image sequence, compiled and exported in a .gif-format. Making animated graphics this way enabled adjustments in animation speed and duration.
22

4.7.2 IMPLEMENTING IN THE CONCEPT CAR The implementation in this iteration was made possible by an administrative software built by one of the engineers at Volvo Cars. This enabled the HUD unit to be used as a projector and controlled manually. Before testing graphics in the car, they were tested on a webpage using the code language React. React is a programming framework used to create user interfaces (UIs). It is component-based, and the basis of the framework is JavaScript (React, 2022). The code was built by one of the software engineers at Volvo Cars. Testing the graphics in both simulations and the car enabled minor design, size, and color adjustments before performing user evaluations.
4.8 FIRST ITERATION - EVALUATE
In the first iteration, the evaluation of graphics design aimed to see how drivers understood the visual information presented to them when operating the car. Nine people who works at Volvo Cars participated in the evaluation. The goal was to understand how users perceive different design concepts and ultimately indicate what design is most easily understandable, desirable, and visible. The test was held operating the concept car, and the users were presented with the three concepts, different colors, and animated graphics. Before the evaluation, the users got to answer formal questions about their driving experience, navigation experience, and if they have used HUD before.
4.8.1 COGNITIVE TASK ANALYSIS Cognitive task analysis (CTA) was performed to understand how users cognitively understand the design concepts. Think-aloud was used to let users verbally express their thoughts during the CTA.
In real-life environments, human processing is a continuous process of understanding and finding the best solutions to perform specific tasks (Rosli, Alias, & Rahman, 2016). Perceiving clear information and feedback from a User Interface (UI) helps users make ideal decisions. CTA is an exceptional method to use when aiming to analyze and understand participants' cognitive processes while performing tasks with a system (Rosli, et al., 2016). Gordon & Gill (1997) say no single definition of what could be evaluated with the CTA method, but it generally involves novices or experts performing tasks. One way to use CTA is to let participants verbalize their thoughts while performing tasks in a realistic-, task-realistic, or context-realistic environment (Gordon & Gill, 1997).
The think-aloud method is performed with participants talking aloud when performing tasks, and the method originates from research on cognitive processes (Jaspers, Steen, Van Den Bos, & Geenen, 2004). Jaspers et al. (2004) suggests that the method is practical because it is easier for people to verbally report their thoughts when performing the task than describe a thought process separately. They mean it is a direct method of gaining insight into the human problem-solving process. To generalize participants, subjects to think-aloud should be within an unspecific area of expertise (Van Someren, Bernard, & Sandberg, 1994).
In this evaluation, CTA was performed using think-aloud as the reporting method for verbally expressing the understood actions. The test track did not enable drivers to make the actions, although putting them in a realistic environment maneuvering the car. All concepts had four different actions displayed to the driver: turn left, take the third exit in the roundabout, change lane to the right, and hold the lane placed to the right. While driving
23

around the test track, these variations were shown: one concept and four actions per lap. When graphics appeared, users were instructed to say aloud what action the graphics were telling them to do. If correct action was expressed, that graphic got one point in the evaluation form, see Appendix B. If the user misunderstood the action, that task got zero points. The learnability of the order of graphics presented was a known risk. To eliminate this, the concept order presented was counterbalanced. Concept one was shown first in the first test and then last in the second test, in the middle in the third. This way, three variations of the order were used.
4.8.2 LIKERT SCALES Likert scales were used to collect data of the participants' opinions on different aspects of the design concepts.
Likert scales are often used in research when the desired data is people’s beliefs, opinions, or affection (Chimi & Russel, 2009). The evaluation method consists of a question about a feeling, belief, opinion, or effect, followed by a scale of responses. These responses commonly range between strongly disagreeing with the statement and strongly agreeing. In between the extreme opposites, the test subject often gets a gradient of options with less extreme responses and a neutral selection. Having a neutral choice can become the default response when participants do not understand the question or feel rushed into choosing to proceed with the next statement (Chimi & Russel, 2009). Whether Likert scales should have a neutral choice or not have opposite opinions among researchers, but research testing these opinions has shown both positive and negative results from the two alternatives (Garland, 1991). Likert scales with stand-alone and unique questions are called Likert-type questions, and when questions are formulated as one main question with a statement for the questions, it is called a Likert scale question (Boone & Boone, 2012).
For this evaluation, six-step Likert-type scales were used. Two questions were regarding how understandable and desirable the graphics in the different concepts were. The third question was regarding the visibility towards the outside environment. The scales ranged from zero to five, with zero being a very negative response and five being a very positive response. The six-step scales did not allow a neutral choice intended to get responses either leaning toward the negative or positive side of the scale, see the design of scales in Appendix B.
4.9 FIRST ITERATION – ANALYZE
Different methods were used when analyzing evaluation results. Since the evaluation collected mainly qualitative and some quantitative data, it was not statistically heavy.
4.9.1 ANALYZING CTA Analyzing the CTA, the overall score was summarized for each concept comparing the three concepts for each of the four actions displayed. Since a correctly expressed action got one point and an incorrect action got zero, the overall score for each action and concept indicated whether users had an easier or harder time understanding what action the graphics communicated. The counterbalancing of concept order could result in different CTA scores depending on the order concepts were shown.
24

4.9.2 ANALYZING LIKERT-TYPE DATA In most statistics, mean values and the standard deviation are used for analyzing data sets, but these values can have unclear meaning when analyzing Likert-type data (Sullivan & Artino, 2013). It is because when analyzing data and getting a mean value between “well” and “very well” it is difficult to find the meaning of a mean value of “well and a half”. Another aspect of not using averages for Likert-type data is extreme values. If one user group's responses were “not at all good”, while the other found it “very good”, responses are extreme opposites, but the average will still show a value in the middle, which is not fairly categorizing the data (Sullivan & Artino, 2013). Analyzing Likert-type should instead have the central tendency as the median and/or modal value (Boone & Boone, 2012). The median value shows the response in the middle of a set, and the modal shows the most popular response. Frequency is the value to analyze for variability for different sets. It gives meaning to the analyzed data and is related to the initial responses (Boone & Boone, 2012).
The results for each concept were compared with median values in this analysis. Analysis of the response frequencies was compared to get other associative differences and see indications of which concept had more positive responses and, were therefore thought better of by the users.
4.10 SECOND ITERATION – RE-DESIGN
The second iteration became shorter due to uncertainties about the concept car´s functionality. Therefore, this phase of the project had to be done on a shorter timeframe and more intense workload.
The iteration started with the re-design/refinements of the graphics based on results from the first iteration. This work was done digitally in Adobe Illustrator, where minor adjustments were made to the graphics. Depending on what situations the graphics were aimed for, combinations of concepts were considered. Animated graphics were re-worked based on the feedback from the evaluation regarding animation speed and duration.
4.11 SECOND ITERATION – IMPLEMENT
Implementing the re-designed graphics, the code used was built by a software engineer at Volvo Cars. The code had the same base code as the simulations in section 4.7.2. The code had to be modified to enable switching between projecting graphics in the traditional HUD field and the AR-HUD field, see Figure 13.
Figure 13: The two projection fields in the AR-HUD system used in concept car.
