When implementing the graphics and testing in the car, the system was tested on the internal test track, making minor adjustments in the code. Coordinates from the GPS-signals in the concept car were used to trigger graphics in the script. The test route was then defined using these coordinates of a geographical positions, defining where and when to project information graphics. This implementation were two lists that the computer went through while driving. One list for the AR-field and one for traditional navigation in the lower field, see Figure 14.
Figure 14: Explanation of how the test route was set up in the car.
Using coordinates to implement the test route meant that the routes were predefined and not dynamic, meaning that ending up off the routes resulted in the guidance stops. In the AR field, graphics developed during this project guided drivers toward their destination. The lower field projected turn-by-turn (TBT) graphics from an open-source provider displaying classic arrows found in existing navigation systems (Mapbox, 2022). The graphics displayed in the AR-field projected graphics at a perceived image depth of 8 to 10 meters. The TBT graphics were projected at the perceived image depth of 2 to 3 meters (Envisics, 2022). Limit value was created by defining an entry- and exit distance to the target, see Figure 14. It enabled customizing the duration the graphics should be shown for, and what distance before a turn or roundabout the graphics should appear. These modifications demanded extensive trials driving the test routes to ensure that the information was presented in time to make the correct actions to the destination.
