4.12 SECOND ITERATION – EVALUATE
The second evaluation aimed to understand driver behavior and the effects of using navigation in AR-HUD while driving in live traffic on the roads of Gothenburg. The evaluation aimed to investigate if differences could be observed comparing driving behavior from gaining navigation information in the two projection fields in the HUD. The evaluation also collected quantitative and qualitative data of the users’ experience interacting with the system. This data was collected with a questionnaire given to users after the test, see Appendix C. The questionnaire was structured similarly to the first evaluation utilizing Likert scale questions.
4.12.1 OBSERVATION Observations gain an understanding of how interaction cues are perceived by users interacting with the product (the perception) and how they understand the interaction (the cognition) (van Boeijen, Daalhuizen, Zijlstra, & van der Schoor, 2017). These aspects result in understanding how perception and cognition of the interaction help users achieve their goals, both intended and unintended interaction. A simple qualitative evaluation needs at least four to ten participants, and documentation of the evaluation should be made with video, audio, or photos for post analysis (van Boeijen et al., 2017).
The plan was to observe users during and after the drive, analyzing video recordings of the users driving the cars. The recordings captured audio and video of the user's driving, enabling analysis of their behavior and see where their attention was focused. The audio recorded could capture thoughts expressed about the interaction during the drive. Appropriate safety checks/actions and general behavior were analyzed from the video of users operating the car.
To compare when information was given in the two projection fields and what effect it had on user attention, the number of glances down in either the near field or driver information module (DIM) was observed. The number of times users directed their attention from the road could then indicate whether information gained in AR-HUD contributes to safer driving.
4.13 SECOND ITERATION – ANALYZE
In this phase, the questionnaire results and videos were analyzed. The scale- and qualitative data were analyzed the same was as in the first iteration by comparing mean values and frequencies of response to the qualitative data gathered.
The videos were visually analyzed where general driving behavior was observed and compared between the two test routes. It showed potential differences when operating the car and if differences in attention can be observed. These observations included whether drivers checked their surroundings before acting to ensure they performed actions safely and did not blindly trust the system.
Visual analysis comparing glances down in the near-field HUD or DIM and focusing on ARfield or the road was performed. The differences are small, but when focusing on the AR field or the road, participant’s eyes focus like in the left picture in Figure 15. When looking for information in the near-field or DIM, the drivers' eyes are focused further down, see the right picture in Figure 15.
27

Figure 15: The small difference in eyes focusing on road and near-field.
Visually analyzing the two routes and counting the number of glances, user attention using different projection fields could be compared. Both routes had the speed displayed in the near-field and DIM, which made gathering information about current speed the same for the two routes. The aim was observing differences of the time focusing on the road when guidance was given in AR-field compared to near-field.
4.14 METHOD DISCUSSION
Reflecting on the beginning of the project, the context immersion could have benefited from interactions and interviews with users. The competitor analysis and try it yourself provided a sufficient understanding of possible user needs. Not including other people when defining user needs was a choice influenced by previous experience from researching AR-HUD that showed it was hard to interview users and get relatable data due to unfamiliarity with the technology. At Volvo Cars, there are certainly users familiar with AR-HUD technology which could have been possible subjects for interviews. However, the risk would be that these people would give biased answers since the project collaborates with Volvo Cars. Results from interviews would have been interesting to see whether the interview subject's needs would agree with the needs found during this project.
The workshop held during ideation should have been more carefully planned because the time given during Brainstorming was too short for some participants. According to Wikberg Nilsson et al. (2015), Brainstorming should be kept short enough for creativity not to go dry. The time given throughout the Brainstorming session was making the participants pressured to come up with ideas, preventing ideas from flowing. The time given did not affect all participants but rather the ones unfamiliar with attending creative workshops. Wikberg Nilsson et al. (2015) mean that reflecting on what people to invite is essential for a successful workshop. The sessions might have been more successful if the workshop was not planned to satisfy the invited people’s schedules.
Finding people to join the different phases of the project was a reoccurring struggle during the project. During evaluations, the people available to participate were Volvo employees, which did not diversify the areas of expertise among participants. When think-aloud is used as a reporting method, Van Someren et al. (1994) express the importance of having unspecified areas of expertise among participants. Having only Volvo employees meant that
28

all participants had some knowledge of the automotive industry, creating a specific area of expertise. Evaluating user expereinces was a challenging task during the project since the expereince of an interaction with a product is specific to each individual. The CTA method utilizing Think-aloud as the reporting method was therefore a great method to use. As Rosli et al. (2016) mean that CTA is a exeptional method for understanding the users cognitive process while interacting with a system the method provided great results of the users experiences which could be analysed based on consistent criterias.
29

