Observing the driving behavior indicated that all participants could operate the vehicle calmly and safely. Indications of participants not scanning the surroundings as frequently were observed when driving with near-field navigation, including checking their mirrors regularly and scanning the road ahead. When graphics appeared, observations indicated that participants needed to take several glances in the near-field before understanding and to perform the action. Their eye could also be observed focusing and re-focusing when transferring between the projection fields. These
47

aspects could not be observed when navigation was given in the AR-field because no eye movements could be observed from the recordings.
The evaluation did not consider reporting whether the users reached their destination without being corrected. During two tests, there was one specific position on the AR-field route where the participant did not understand the action needed to proceed on the set route. This position was after a roundabout when information to take a turning lane was given immediately when exiting. The test leader corrected the test to proceed. These corrections were few compared to the near-field route, where correction was made more times due to participants not seeing or understanding the graphics projected in the near-field.
5.3 SUMMARY OF RESULTS
This section of the report gives a summary of the results to present an overview before proceeding with the discussion chapter.
- Concept A, designed with arrows providing visual information of actions to make in traffic, was the most understandable and desirable design to implement regarding static turn-by-turn graphics.
- When designing navigation graphics, what color to use resulted in cyan being the most appreciated color for having a neutral meaning, good visibility, not being too bright, and inviting to actions.
- Graphics communicating lane changes resulted in concept C being a well-understood representation of the action needed. The graphics were animated slower and with stops at the beginning and end, making it easier to understand. The lane elements from the “holding lane graphics” were used for the re-design lane change graphic.
- Implementation into a realistic driving environment resulted in a predefined route using the vehicle’s GPS coordinates to trigger graphics. Adjustments for the duration of graphics showing resulted in a good implementation for users to reach their destinations.
- Seven test routes resulted in shorter tests due to system failures which were adjusted for by calculating the number of glances per second of driving, resulting in fair comparisons between driving routes.
- Likert scale data from the driving evaluation resulted in a positive response distribution for the user’s experiences of operating the vehicle safely and how useful the information was. Response distribution for the timing of graphics resulted in more negative response frequencies indicating that timing is a critical factor when implementing the graphics. Results from qualitative feedback also characterized timing as a critical factor.
- Observations of video recordings indicated that drivers are more aware of the situation outside the vehicle when presented with information in the AR-field. Observation also indicated that AR navigation aided participants in keeping their eyes on the road more frequently rather than searching for information in near-field or DIM.
48

6 DISCUSSION
This section discusses the project results and their relevance to the theory presented in the theoretical framework. Reflections about the results and the process are presented together with conclusions where the objectives and aims of the project are discussed, research questions answered, and recommendations for future work are given.
6.1 POSITIONING THE RESULT
At the beginning of this project, the goal was to explore perceptual effects using AR-HUD. This included researching the perceptual aspects of the interactions and evaluating whether safe driving could be performed using AR-HUD. As Persson & Warell (2003) suggests that product development needs to consider human needs and user experiences, and De Vere et al. (2010) imply that Industrial design engineers should be able to perform a complete product development process. The project shifted toward exploring graphics design in the first iteration and perceptual aspects in AR-HUD navigation in the second.
Exploring different designs of navigation graphics was no easy task to create ideas different from arrows generally used in cars. With navigation being a standard driver's aid, the people involved in ideation were bound to the idea of arrows displaying wayfinding information. ARHUD is also a new and somewhat unexplored technology, meaning there were no set guidelines for designing graphics for navigation features. As Krippendorff (1989) says that design is making sense of things, the graphics explored in the project evaluated if wayfinding information could be understood and perceived with design principles based on cognitive psychology. Norman (2013) stresses the importance of HMI design in creating a system with humans and machines in symbiosis with clear communication between each other. Even though results from evaluating graphics design showed arrows being perceived best and preferred by users, it was necessary to explore alternative ways of designing navigation graphics.
The need for local guidance indicating the vehicle's position, and at the same time having turn-by-turn (TBT) information, showed to be a reasonable amount of information to implement. The two types of information were aimed for different situations in traffic: positioning to give information going toward intersection and TBT when at the intersections. Hassenzahl & Tractinsky (2006) proposes that the shift in user experience design has made users' expectations an essential to consider. Having AR-HUD navigation display positioning of the vehicle before intersections created the possibility for the users' expectations to be processed before the TBT graphics. This could have the effect of users already knowing what actions are possible in the near future, making information easier to perceive and understand.
The project's typical user was challenging to define working with a commonly used system as navigation. Therefore, the project began exploring general ideas of creating an AR-HUD navigation interface. Since the users were mainly involved in the evaluations and because the time frame only allowed two iterations, further work is still needed to define the user needs most important to consider. As Norman (2013) suggest that iterative design is repeated until results are satisfactory, this project would have needed a few more iterations regarding the design of information and better defining user needs for what information to include.
49

6.2 RELEVANCE
In today’s automotive industry, AR-HUDs still has development left to reach the potential benefits it could bring to the people in traffic environments. Traditional HUDs are implemented by many automotive manufacturers and do a sufficient job of providing drivers with navigation information close to where they are focusing on the road. This project's results indicated clear differences in where the driver’s attention was focused, comparing AR-HUD and traditional HUD. As Ojsteršek & Topolšek (2019) say, distractions of the driver’s attention make driving performance suffer and could ultimately cause accidents. Since cars today have multiple sources of information, an AR-HUD is an excellent system for displaying the most relevant navigation information about the traffic environment at the correct time. Endlsey et al. (2003) say that being aware of the situation is vital when making correct decisions in traffic. Therefore, if developed with user needs and cognitive aspects considered, both Norman (2013) and Endsley et al. (2003) say that errors are not the user's fault, but rather the user not understanding the information. AR-HUD could make the interaction with navigation guidance more effortless and less stressful for the cognitive process of visual information processing. If driving can be done more effortlessly and correctly, safety in driving environments benefits drivers, pedestrians, and Volvo Cars.
Volvo Cars are known for producing safe cars and work toward zero people being killed or seriously injured operating their vehicles (Volvo Cars, n.d.). These facts and the automotive industries evolving toward autonomous driving make effortless information exchanges between machines and users essential. When using navigation, the interaction with the car is operating the vehicle towards a set destination. A well-designed and implemented ARHUD navigation could benefit Volvo Cars by creating safer driving in their vehicles and more enjoyable interactions, since Hassenzahl & Tractinsky (2006) proposes that the user experience has shifted beyond the interaction and today also includes emotional, experiential, and affectional aspects. Volvo’s vehicle development could therefore benefit from this project's results, indicating that drivers had their eyes and attention more towards the road when using AR-HUD navigation compared to traditional HUD navigation. Results also indicated that users had a more enjoyable experience when using AR-HUD, making Volvo’s cars perceived as both safe and enjoyable to drive.
6.3 REFLECTION
The chosen process and plan for this have been helpful during the development. The process gave clear direction of the stages and what each stage should result in. Uncertainties occurred throughout the whole process, and the initial plan and process gave good guidance on how to proceed. The plan for the project was initially a bit optimistic, having three iterations, but by trusting the process of iterations and making reasonable adjustments, the project results were satisfactory.
Reflecting on the project process, parts of it had unnecessary time spent on work not contributing as much as the time spent on it. At the beginning of the project, much time was spent thinking about the process and how to include users during the context immersion. Since the UX design processes typically emphasize user interviews to define needs, it was challenging to figure out how to define user needs for AR-HUD. The previous project working with AR-HUD influenced a lot because that project proved it difficult not to formulate too leading interview questions. Answers were not often relatable to AR-HUD when making open questions about AR-HUD. This was why initial interviews were thought of but later removed,
50

taking unnecessary time. Time was also spent learning the coding language and understanding the implementation in the concept vehicle. Some of the time and effort were necessary and made the results what they are, but time spent thinking that understanding the code could be managed without consulting a software engineer could have been spent on other parts of the process.
Events surrounding the project that could not be controlled happened, resulting in process adjustments. One event was when the concept car stopped working, creating uncertainties for the whole second iteration. With just a couple of weeks left of the thesis, the software engineers at Volvo Cars got the car to work again, resulting in quick adjustments to the second iteration. When the car did not work, time was spent thinking of how the evaluation of driving behavior could be done without the concept car. A quickly created test plan had to be designed when it started working again resulting in a sufficient plan, but with more time it could have been more thought through and detailed.
6.4 CONCLUSIONS
When beginning this thesis project, the objective and aims were established. Three research questions were also formulated to be answered during the project.
6.4.1 PROJECT OBJECTIVE AND AIMS The objective for this project was:
“To research, ideate, implement and evaluate a user interface design for a navigation feature in an AR-HUD. Existing technological limitations, human perception, and cognitive psychology are considered throughout the development process to develop a feasible concept to implement and evaluate.”
The aim of the project was:
“To design and evaluate the user interaction in an AR-HUD to achieve good user experiences and evaluate whether AR-HUD contributes to safer driving. The aim could be divided into two parts: part one aim to evaluate visual information designs. The second part evaluates user experience with drivers operating an AR-HUD car regarding perception, attention, and safety. The project results aim to contribute with valuable insights for future research within the subject of AR-HUDs from an industrial design engineering perspective.”
6.4.2 RESEARCH QUESTIONS At the beginning of the project, three research questions were defined to be answered based on the results.
“How can information be designed and implemented to be perceived correctly and safely in an AR-HUD?”
The most critical aspect for information to be perceived correctly and safely was the timing of when graphics appear when driving. As Gibson (1979) explains in his theory about ecological perception, humans actively explore their environment, searching to understand how to interact with it. The theory suggests that humans explore objects and events in the
51

environments, and indications regarding the timing of graphics could relate to this theory. The results indicated that drivers had their attention on the road most of the time, perceiving the road ahead. Knowing they should follow a set route with guidance from the car, when information did not appear when they expected it, they started actively searching for information to base their next decision on. Endsley et al. (2003) say that it is crucial to keep the user in control over the system simultaneously as having high levels of situation awareness. Results indicated that when information was absent or came too late, the users displayed behavior of not being in control and knowing where to go. When uncertainties occurred, they could either trust that the system would provide necessary information or search the road ahead and base their actions on driving experience. Providing users with information at the correct time can ease cognitive load while driving and eliminate uncertainties of what actions to take.
Exploring how to design graphics was an essential part of this project, resulting in the arrows being easily understood for turn-by-turn information. This result could indicate that knowledge from arrows being commonly used in navigation systems made them correctly understood. This indication could relate to the constructive perception theory (Gregory, 2015), implying that humans perceive information based on previous knowledge and constructing mental models of what the information means. It would mean that based on them knowing that arrows are used for indicating directions, they understood the graphics easier and more correctly than the horizontal lines or the rectangles. Users also found the arrows more desirable. This could relate to the possibility of encoding information from features (Ware, 2019) and relating the information to memories in long-term memory (Wickens et al., 2013). This process could have made arrows more easily understood and desirable because they could relate to the arrows easily and intuitively understand what they meant. When designing the horizontal line and the rectangle concept, the ideas were related to gestalt grouping principles. Both concepts were designed with similarity and common fate (Lidwell et al., 2010) to be perceived as grouped elements moving in the same direction. When perceiving these concepts compared to the arrows, understanding the information might be more demanding when they could not be related to past knowledge.
Lateral movement was better understood with animated graphics resulting in the rectangle concept with the rectangle animating either left or right. Common fate implies that moving elements toward static elements are perceived easier (Lidwell et al., 2010), which could have made understanding the lateral action easier. The graphic also had lane indications clarifying that a lane change was called for. The animated lane change graphics was understood easily, but the animation could also attract too much attention. According to Osvalder & Ulfvengren (2015), the information attended to is the most prominent and clear information most easily gathered, and animated graphics should therefore be used with care when projected in the driver's field of view.
Exploring colors used in AR-HUD, a blue cyan nuance was best perceived by users. This color can be the best color to use due to users having no perceived meaning for blue compared to green, yellow, and red which are colors already used a lot in vehicles and traffic, e.g., warning signals and traffic signals. White was perceived as almost as good as cyan, but white can blend with road lines since the graphics are projected toward the road. A blue nuance could also blend in with the sky or water, but the cyan nuance distinguishes it from a clear blue. The colors were not evaluated with users having color deficiencies during the evaluation because of difficulties gathering participants with deficiencies to get an appropriate result.
52

These project results cannot tell if cyan is a suitable color for users with color deficiencies, but the colors were evaluated with one participant with deficiencies. The participants results were excluded due to their involvement in the SCREENS project, but the results indicated that cyan was the most easily perceived color which aligns with the other results.
“What information is suitable for drivers regarding a navigation feature in an AR-HUD?”
The implemented information was suitable to guide the participants to their destination during the evaluations. Additional information was suggested after the end of the test, e.g., distances to turns. Osvalder & Ulfvengren (2015) say that the amount of information is critical for what information is attended to and how much effort is needed to process the information. This made the amount of information an essential aspect considered and why the concept had information appear and disappear with appropriate timing, rather than always being visible. The main idea of AR-HUD was to make virtual information co-exist with the real environment and therefore the information was almost solely represented by graphical elements with the least amount of text possible. The desire to have the distance to turn information indicated that it in some way should be considered to clarify the distances. The implementation in this project limited the graphics from augmenting in size, which could make the position of the actions clearer. The graphics implementation needs further development to accommodate different driving situations when proofing this concept. Turnby-turn information should be customizable to specific situations, e.g., angles of turns and layout of roundabouts.
The most prominent indication of suitable information to include in AR-HUD is the vehicle's positioning. The implementation was animated graphics that users positively received. Easily perceived information of where the vehicle should be positioned on the road at the present time, was indicated to be appropriate information in AR-HUD. It relates to Endsley et al. (2003) saying that situation awareness of the surrounding in the present and near future is an essential aspect of decision-making. This information was easily understandable and could help users make safe and correct decisions in traffic, increasing their performance and assurance of being on the correct route towards their destination. This information could also ease driving with multiple lanes exist and the cognitive load is high to understand where to position the car. It would apply to situations like highways where traffic is hectic, and a lot of stimuli must be attended to. This information could be particularly helpful for novice drivers when navigating their way in chaotic traffic environments.
“How does AR-HUD affect driver attention and distraction?”
The main purpose of using AR-HUD is to enable drivers to keep their eyes on the road and at the same time, gain important information from what comes ahead. Observing users operating the car and seeing the effects AR-HUD navigation and traditional HUD navigation had on attention and distraction, the results were clear that AR graphics distracted the user less than a traditional HUD. Observations indicated that users scanned their surroundings more frequently when using AR graphics, promoting safer driving because of higher levels of situation awareness. Although this project's evaluation resulted in indications favoring AR navigation, there could also be risks with projecting graphics in the driver’s field of view. If graphics are designed to
53

attract too much attention and are too prominent toward the environment, it could distract the driver creating dangerous situations. Therefore, the technological limitation of the system used in the concept car could be beneficial because of the limited area of the windshield possible to project on.
The observation technique was affected by the human factor that could have made the analyses inconsistent throughout the analyses, therefore the results are referred to as indications in this project.
6.5 RECOMMENDATIONS
The concept created was a predefined implementation of a set route with a small sample of participants. The future work would firstly be recommended to evaluate the concept with a more larger sample size, including a more even distribution of male and female participants. Furthermore, the evaluation would also benefit from having users outside of Volvo Cars evaluate the concept to avoid biased results. Statistical significance of the results could then be seen, verifying the benefits of using AR-HUD. Also, if an eye-tracking device would be used, the eye movements analyzed by observing video recordings in this project could possibly be proven scientifically.
The information given in this concept was limited to specific actions, and for future development work, creating a dynamic system would include creating a more extensive library of graphics. A dynamic system could also consider other safety features creating a redundant system with several driving aid functions working together, resulting in safer driving. This way, meaningful information about the surroundings could be presented in the field of view, creating a high level of situation awareness and less cognitive load when making decisions.
Graphic designs evaluated in this project could be further perfected because of the importance of clear information presented in the correct situation. The best graphics to use in AR-HUD are graphics that are easily perceived and understood, not drawing too much attention, and possible graphic designs need to be further evaluated. Future development should also consider color deficiencies to create a system that can be used by as many people as possible.
This project followed a human-centered approach which is highly recommended for future development work. Indications from the evaluations with users showed a clear result of all users having different experiences from driving the car equipped with AR-HUD. Therefore, the user experience and the interaction between the user and system is recommended as the most critical aspect. This project also showed that the qualitative data from evaluation is as vital as measurable data. Having a good balance of the types of data collected is therefore important for a complete overview of the users’ experience interacting with the system.
